id: pm-qxo5iu
title: "Sentry exception capture never writes the pm.error_code and pm.exit_code tags the release gate reads, so every unexpected runtime error is unclassifiable by construction and blocks the daily cut for the full rolling window"
description: "The release reliability gate was rebound to canonical error codes (pm-dqtzva), and it reads pm.error_code plus pm.exit_code from Sentry issue tags, failing closed when either is absent. The exception producer never satisfies that contract. sentryCaptureCliError calls captureException with an extra bag only: exit_code and error_context land in event extras, which the gate does not read, and no tag is written at capture time. The only writer of those tags is setSentryCommandFinishTags, reached through sentryFinishCommandSpan, which begins with an early return when no command span is active. Two consequences follow and both are realized. Ordering: in the unknown-error path the capture runs before the finish call, so the scope tags do not exist yet when the event is sent. Span dependence: any failure raised before or outside an active command span never reaches the tag writer at all, so no ordering fix alone can satisfy the contract. The result is a producer/consumer split in which the gate demands a contract the reporter cannot emit for the exact class of events the gate exists to judge. Every unexpected runtime fault therefore enters the window as unclassifiable, fails closed, and blocks every scheduled release until it ages out of --sentry-window-days or a human resolves it by hand. The 2026-08-08 scheduled cut failed this way on two untagged handled events. Duplicate check searched sentry exception capture tags, captureException pm.error_code, and command span gating across all statuses; pm-9iho covers console-integration leakage and pm-dqtzva covers the consumer side, neither owns the producer tag contract."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[6]: "area:ci","area:observability",error-taxonomy,gate,release,sentry
created_at: "2026-08-08T07:48:05.769Z"
